  <data name="CurveExplanation" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The curve y^2 = x^3 + {0}x + {1} modulo {2} has the points drawn below and the point at infinity O ({3} points in all). Two points are added with a line through them: the third intersection point, reflected on the x axis, is the sum.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CurveLegend" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Red: P, blue: Q, green: P + Q. The white ring is the third intersection point of the line through P and Q (modulo p the line wraps around, so only the points are exact).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CurveNoPoints" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Give two points x1 y1 x2 y2 on the curve to see their sum.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CurveSum" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>P = ({0}, {1}) + Q = ({2}, {3}) = {4}</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CurveTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Elliptic curve over a prime field</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ErrorTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The parameters cannot be used</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ExamplesText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>RSA:  p q e m             61 53 17 65
Shamir:  p n a0 a1 ...    23 5 11 3 7
Elliptic curve:  p a b [x1 y1 x2 y2]    17 2 2 5 1 6 3</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ExamplesTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Examples of parameters</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ParametersCaption" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Parameters</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ParametersTooltip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Numbers separated by spaces. RSA: p q e m. Shamir: p n a0 a1 ... Elliptic curve: p a b, and optionally x1 y1 x2 y2.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PluginCaption" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Math Visualization</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PluginTooltip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shows RSA, Shamir secret sharing and elliptic curve arithmetic with small numbers</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ResultCaption" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Result</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ResultTooltip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>RSA: the ciphertext c. Shamir: the reconstructed secret. Elliptic curve: the point P + Q as "x,y", or O.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RsaExplanation" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Two primes p and q give the modulus n and phi(n). The public exponent e must be coprime to phi(n); the private exponent d is its inverse modulo phi(n). Encryption raises the message to the power e, decryption raises the result to the power d, modulo n.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RsaPrivateKey" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Private key</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RsaPublic" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>public</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RsaPublicKey" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Public key</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RsaTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>RSA with small numbers</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ShamirExplanation" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The polynomial f(x) = {0} modulo {1} has degree {2} - 1, so {2} points are needed to find it; the constant term is the secret. {3} shares (x, f(x)) are handed out.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ShamirLegend" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Grey: all points (x, f(x)) of the polynomial modulo p. Orange: the shares. Green: the secret f(0).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ShamirReconstructed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Secret from the first {0} shares (Lagrange)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ShamirShare" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Share</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ShamirTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shamir's secret sharing over a small prime field</value>
  </data>
